  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do spiders straighten their legs?",
      "a": "Spiders raise the pressure of their blood by squeezing their front body, and the fluid pushes into the legs and forces the joints straight. Two main joints in each leg have no muscle to do this, so fluid pressure does the job.",
      "asks": [
        "Do spiders use hydraulics?",
        "How do spider legs work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do dead spiders curl up?",
      "a": "A living spider keeps its legs straight with blood pressure. When it dies the pressure is lost, so the muscles and joints that bend the legs pull them in, leaving the legs curled under the body.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do spider legs curl when they die?",
        "Why is a dead spider all scrunched up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is haemolymph?",
      "a": "Haemolymph is a spider's blood. A long heart in the abdomen pumps it, and it flows freely around the organs rather than only in closed vessels. It carries oxygen using a copper-based chemical that turns it bluish.",
      "asks": [
        "What colour is spider blood?",
        "Do spiders have blood?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do jumping spiders jump?",
      "a": "A jumping spider fixes a silk safety line, then straightens its back legs very fast, helped by a surge of blood pressure, and leaps many times its own body length onto prey.",
      "asks": [
        "How far can a jumping spider jump?",
        "Why do jumping spiders use silk when they jump?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do spiders have muscles?",
      "a": "Yes, many: to bend their legs, work their fangs and pull silk. They only lack muscles to straighten two main joints in each leg, which is where fluid pressure takes over.",
      "asks": [
        "Are spider legs all muscle?",
        "How strong are spider muscles?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Spider legs lack extensor muscles at two major joints and are straightened by haemolymph pressure (Parry and Brown, 1959).",
    "Spider blood carries oxygen with haemocyanin, a copper-based molecule that looks blue when it carries oxygen."
  ],
